                <mxCell id="8" value="Becuase Strings are actually objects that are represented using class files like the car class that we learned about in lesson 4 and 5, we need to use a method (aka the &quot;.equals()&quot; method) instead of a comparison operator to compare two strings together. This means we can't use the == sign to compare Strings. The reason for this is because Strings are objects and when we use the == sign on objects it compares memory address's, not the information stored inside of the memory address. Thus we need a method that tells java to actually compare the data inside of the objects not the memory address's. The reason we don't need this on like integers or double is because they are what are called primitive data types and are much simpler than objects like a String or a Car object and Java can easily define the comparison functionality for this type of information.&amp;nbsp;" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;" vertex="1" parent="1">
                    <mxGeometry x="560" y="40" width="270" height="260" as="geometry"/>
                </mxCell>
